return [====[for a quarter-second, then you charge straight along your original aim for up to three blocks in 0.8 seconds. You cannot steer. Walls, locked gates, actors, stairs, ledges, wet or harmful spaces stop the charge. Start on a supported flat floor with at least 32 units of safe room.</p><p>The first enemy contact deals one hit of 2d8 plus Wisdom as electric Magic, with its normal Morale rider. Surviving enemies that lose HP may suffer ordinary hit-stun, subject to their existing immunities and retrigger rules. Allies stop your charge without taking damage. There is no piercing or splash.</p><p>The charge grants no invulnerability, flight or extra Dodge. It uses ordinary offensive Magic cost modifiers and reveals Veil. Failed activation is free; once committed, interruption or a miss does not refund Magic or reset the cooldown. Removal, death, role or dungeon changes, forced movement, Held or Muted cancel it.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 37</div></article><article class="page" id="heavy-plumber" data-title="Boots of the Heavy Plumber"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Boots of the Heavy Plumber</h2><table><tr><td>Heavy Stomp</td><td>Land from above · Passive · No Magic cost</td></tr></table><p>These Rare-or-better boots occupy your feet slot. Equip them before leaving solid ground, then land on an enemy from above to deal 2d6 plus Strength as physical damage. Your usual dice, defenses and damage modifiers apply. Sideways contact and landing on allies do not attack.</p><p>A valid stomp gives you a short upward rebound, even if the enemy dodges or blocks the damage. Low ceilings reduce the rebound. With insufficient clearance or unsafe floor support below, you land normally without an attack. The Boots do not grant flight, invulnerability or protection from fall damage.</p><p>You get one stomp attempt per airborne excursion, with at least 0.65 seconds between commitments. Your bounce is part of that same excursion. Land on solid, non-actor support to rearm; standing on an enemy or swapping Boots in midair will not do it.</p><p>Heavy Stomp needs no arrow sequence or Magic. Muted and holding a potion do not disable it; Held does. A committed stomp reveals Veil. Removing the Boots or changing life, role or dungeon invalidates the armed landing.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 38</div></article><article class="page" id="magic-hourglass" data-title="A little more time"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>A little more time</h2><table><tr><td>Magic Hourglass</td><td>LMB / RMB: USE · Adds 2d4 minutes · Stack of 3</td></tr></table><p>This rare consumable buys the whole party more time. Equip and hold it in the Throwable slot, then press either mouse button. One Hourglass is consumed; the server rolls two ordinary four-sided dice and adds their sum in minutes to the current dungeon clock. The Die Log shows both dice, and everyone&#x27;s clock updates immediately.</p><p>The result is always two through eight minutes: no exploding dice, class bonuses or Magic cost. Any Hero class can use it, with the ordinary 0.6-second item cooldown. A stack holds at most three, and each legitimate use adds its full result even above 30:00.</p><p>Use it before time runs out. At or after the deadline, TIME OVER wins; the Hourglass cannot reverse the collapse. Paused staging clocks, failed or cleared dungeons, Soldier bodies and unequipped items cannot spend it. Successful rescue resets the next dungeon to 30:00, while unused Hourglasses remain in your Hero&#x27;s inventory.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 39</div></article><article class="page" id="moon-boots" data-title="Boots of the Moon"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Boots of the Moon</h2><table><tr><td>Moon Gravity</td><td>25% lower gravity · Passive · No Magic cost</td></tr></table><p>Equip these Rare-or-better boots in your feet slot for higher jumps and longer airtime in the dungeon. Gravity is reduced by 25%; ordinary steering and horizontal movement remain yours to control. No arrow sequence, Magic cost or cooldown is required.</p><p>Your usual jump, Cloud Step and Wall Jump impulses still apply. Float On keeps its own controls and Magic cost. Invisible maze walls, locked gates, collisions and fall damage remain in force: these boots do not grant flight or invulnerability.</p><p>Removing or replacing the boots restores ordinary gravity, including in midair. Their effect ends on death, staging or role change and returns when the same equipped boots belong to a living, deployed Hero again. Human Soldiers cannot borrow the Hero&#x27;s equipment.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 40</div></article><article class="page" id="tanuki-ring" data-title="Tanuki’s Ring"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Tanuki’s Ring</h2><table><tr><td>Statue</td><td>Stand still for 2 seconds · Passive · No Magic cost</td></tr></table><p>Equip this Rare-or-better ring in either hand and stand still on solid ground for two continuous seconds. You become stone: enemies stop seeing and targeting you, and direct hits, explosions, hazards and damaging statuses cannot take your HP while Statue remains active.</p><p>Movement immediately ends Statue, including jumps, crouching, forced propulsion, moving supports and teleportation. Attacking, casting Magic, using an item or interacting also ends it, even if the action fails. Dealing HP damage ends it too. You must stand still for another full two seconds; looking around alone is allowed.</p><p>Statue costs no Magic and has no fixed duration or cooldown. It does not cure existing conditions or stop the dungeon collapse clock. Removing or moving the ring, dying, changing role or changing dungeon cancels it. An interrupted connection cannot retain an old activation.</p><p>Other players can see your stone body. A brief original transformation chime and stone flourish mark activation, and STATUE appears above your status portrait. Separate sources of invisibility retain their own rules.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 41</div></article><article class="page" id="wand" data-title="A little borrowed sorcery"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>A little borrowed sorcery</h2><table><tr><td>Wand</td><td>LMB: piercing Beam · 12 charges · No reload</td></tr><tr><td>Wizard</td><td>Automatic class permission</td></tr><tr><td>Rogue</td><td>d100 ≤ 5 × Combat Level, capped at95</td></tr><tr><td>Fighter</td><td>May carry; cannot activate</td></tr></table><p>Each Wand is a distinct procedural weapon with its own twelve-charge pool. Equip a copy through Equipment and press LMB to attempt a Beam. One accepted attempt spends one charge and no personal Magic, with at least0.65 seconds between attempts. RMB still casts your selected Spellbook Magic.</p><p>Wizards activate Wands normally. Rogues make one non-exploding d100 roll:5% success at Level1,50% at Level10,95% at Levels19 and20. A failed Rogue attempt still consumes the charge and cooldown but produces no Beam. Other classes cannot activate it. Muted and Intimidated prevent activation without spending a charge.</p><p>A successful Wand Beam deals3d6 plus Wisdom through the shared Magic combat rules. It pierces eligible bodies once each in order and stops at blocking architecture or its normal range, extended by your existing spatial bonuses. Its cyan core keeps the familiar Beam identity; the Wand affinity supplies elemental damage and color accents.</p><p>The Wand uses its rolled equipment status chances and normal saves after effective damage; it does not add a second automatic Content rider. Your normal compatible damage modifiers and defenses still apply. Wand use does not unlock Beam or a Content in the Spellbook, and spending a charge does not count as spending personal Magic.</p><p>The remaining charges are always shown while the Wand is held and in its Equipment description. Empty Wands remain in your inventory. Reloading, ammunition pickups, regeneration, swapping copies, weapon recreation, respawning and dungeon transitions never refill a copy. Stored and frozen copies preserve their recorded charges. Attack attempts reveal Veil and end Statue.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 42</div></article><article class="page" id="death" data-title="Down, but not finished"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Down, but not finished</h2><div role="img" aria-label="Deborah demonstrates" class="art" style="background-size:300% 200%;background-position:50% 100%"></div><p>Heroes start with three lives. On death, your weapons, magazines, reserve ammunition, equipped wearables, stored gear, and throwable potions stay with your Hero for the respawn. Your character identity, XP, class, feats, and persistent DFT collection remain too. Reconnecting does not restore a lost life or reroll your character.</p><p>A normal death has a mandatory 20-second respawn lockout. Play Tetris while waiting, or spectate. After the lockout, press F to finalize your reward and respawn. You can keep playing until the normal 60-second death-Tetris maximum, when respawn happens automatically.</p><p>At zero lives, press F3 for three choices: wait for resurrection, begin a new Level-1 Hero, or join the Soldiers. Beginning again discards that character&#x27;s carried gear and progression but preserves account $DEB and DFTs. All Heroes may be eliminated without resetting the dungeon; only the collapse timer ends a failed run. Someone must still reach the rescue objective to clear it.</p><p>Successful rescue can bring eliminated teammates back under the campaign&#x27;s comeback rules, up to the maximum of four lives. A revived Hero returns with the same saved weapons, ammunition, wearables, stored gear, and throwable potions.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 43</div></article><article class="page" id="tetris" data-title="Make room for a comeback"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Make room for a comeback</h2><table><tr><td>One line</td><td>+10 temporary HP</td></tr><tr><td>Two lines at once</td><td>+30 temporary HP</td></tr><tr><td>Three lines at once</td><td>+50 temporary HP</td></tr><tr><td>Four lines at once</td><td>+80 temporary HP</td></tr></table><p>Move pieces left or right, rotate with Up/Forward, soft-drop with Down/Back, and hard-drop with Jump. Clearing several lines at once earns the corresponding reward, not the sum of separate single-line clears.</p><p>Rewards become temporary HP overfill. They do not permanently increase maximum HP and cannot be refilled by ordinary regeneration. Topping out ends the board session while preserving the bonus already earned; it does not erase the mandatory death wait.</p><p>After rescue, Victory Tetris lasts 20 seconds. Russian Asset doubles qualifying HP rewards in both Tetris modes. It also raises the death-Tetris maximum to 120 seconds, but F still becomes available after the same 20-second lockout. It never extends Victory Tetris or the active dungeon clock.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 44</div></article><article class="page" id="soldiers" data-title="Friends—and human foes"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Friends—and human foes</h2><p>Up to four players take cooperative Hero roles. Up to six additional players can control enemy Soldiers, for ten active players; further arrivals spectate. Role availability and the Soldier queue determine when an incarnation can be entered.</p><p>A human Soldier receives a generated monster character, class, abilities, and automatically selected feats. The player does not draft Hero feats. A fresh incarnation is a fresh Soldier character, not the revival of the previous build.</p><p>Human Soldiers can earn incarnation-local XP and automatic Combat Levels, capped by Dungeon Level +3. AI Soldiers do not earn XP. Monster-side Levels can reach 999. Soldier play preserves your eliminated Hero; return to the Hero Queue to become eligible for resurrection.</p><p>The Debbie Fund can reward Soldier-attributable Hero life losses after a successful rescue. Switching into Soldier play forfeits that player&#x27;s Hero share for that dungeon. The fund does not award a rescue pool for a timeout.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 45</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-basics" data-title="Meet the prison staff"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Meet the prison staff</h2><p>An enemy&#x27;s archetype tells you how it fights; its class, Combat Level, feats, and element make that particular creature different. Normal tier chances are 60% Typical, 30% Elite, and 10% Champion, using Dungeon Level, +1, and +2 respectively. A tier is a Level change, not a secret second set of damage multipliers.</p><p>Shamblers threaten close quarters; Runners close gaps quickly. Deadcrabs are low jumping attackers. Soldiers use ranged fire; Blitzers press aggressively; Snipers punish exposed sight lines.</p><p>BioBlasters threaten with biological attacks. Watchers demand attention to their readable attack tells. Seekers telegraph a committed charge: move out of its path instead of retreating along it.</p><p>Listen, look ahead, and use corners. Killing an enemy does not guarantee a permanently safe floor; wandering populations can replenish. Size, class tint, elemental aura, and aimed information help you decide what to tackle first.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 46</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-new" data-title="More unwelcome company"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>More unwelcome company</h2><table><tr><td>Climber</td><td>Pink fast-zombie silhouette; climbs to reach you. Watch vertical approaches.</td></tr><tr><td>Nodule</td><td>Stationary gas hazard. Do not stand beside it.</td></tr><tr><td>Flamer</td><td>Red soldier silhouette; warns before a short-range fire attack.</td></tr><tr><td>Big Crab</td><td>Oversized headcrab with a telegraphed fire attack.</td></tr><tr><td>Sentry</td><td>Stationary gun emplacement; break its firing line.</td></tr><tr><td>Razor</td><td>Flying blade with a warned dive. Move off its committed approach.</td></tr><tr><td>Arc Caster</td><td>Marks a target area before its Magic strike. Leave the mark.</td></tr><tr><td>Lurker</td><td>Ceiling ambusher with a venom shot. Look up.</td></tr><tr><td>Beam Sweeper</td><td>Stationary sweeping laser. Interrupt its charge or use cover; gunfire will not cancel an already released beam.</td></tr></table><p>Fire can attempt Immolated; venom can attempt Poisoned. These enemies use the same saves, damage, elements, and progression rules as the rest of the roster.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 47</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-control" data-title="The control detail"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>The control detail</h2><table><tr><td>Gaoler</td><td>Icy-blue Vortigaunt. Its Ice attack marks a fixed patch of floor before erupting. Leave the mark or interrupt the charge: a failed Wisdom save can leave you Held while its Runner closes in.</td></tr><tr><td>Silencer</td><td>Pale-ivory Combine elite. A visible line warns of a straight, non-homing Light bolt. Sidestep or take cover. A failed Wisdom save can leave you Muted; guns and melee remain available.</td></tr><tr><td>Repulsor</td><td>Brown-and-gold Vortigaunt. It warns before an Earth pulse around its fixed casting position. Leave the ring or take cover. The hit can push you into a Soldier’s firing line.</td></tr></table><p>These specialists appear with ordinary attackers. Identify the control threat early, then choose whether to interrupt it, evade its commitment, or remove its companion.</p><p>Held and Muted use the ordinary saves and recovery rules. Their warning colors identify the attack; a creature’s class and elemental affinity still belong to that individual. Cover and movement remain useful answers for every class.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 48</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-support" data-title="The prison support detail"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>The prison support detail</h2><table><tr><td>Stitcher</td><td>A slave Vortigaunt channels recovery into one wounded ally. The green link announces a 1.5-second window to interrupt it or break its line of sight. Each completed channel restores 12% of the ally’s maximum HP, rounded up and capped at 24 HP; it cannot revive the dead.</td></tr><tr><td>Bulwark</td><td>A Combine elite protects one ally with a blue tether. Its six-second guard adds 25 percentage points to ordinary physical Block, still capped at 33%. Magic bypasses Block. Kill or interrupt the Bulwark, draw the ally more than 240 units away, or break the tether with cover.</td></tr><tr><td>Cantor</td><td>A Metrocop calls up to three nearby allies onto one visible Hero for four seconds. The gold call changes their target priority without adding damage or speed. Break sightlines, interrupt the caller, or turn the pursuing group into your team’s firing line.</td></tr></table><p>These enemies support existing companions; they neither summon reinforcements nor revive fallen enemies. Support does not stack. When nobody qualifies for support, each specialist can still fire a weak ordinary projectile.</p><p>Their distinctive links and call warnings remain visible with reduced effects. Generated class and elemental affinity are independent of the support role.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 49</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-pursuit" data-title="Watch the other route"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Watch the other route</h2><table><tr><td>Pincer</td><td>A purple Combine soldier takes a legal alternate approach around the lane where it saw you, using a short loop or a two-leg flank within its own room. Its split chevron gives a brief warning before it moves. Watch the side approach, change lanes, or interrupt it; it cannot cross a locked gate or follow an unseen change of direction.</td></tr><tr><td>Harrier</td><td>A cyan Metrocop commits a visible firing line and releases one straight projectile before withdrawing. Its backward chevrons announce the retreat. Dodge the shot, then close the gap or deny its cover; interrupting the wind-up prevents the planned withdrawal.</td></tr><tr><td>Waylayer</td><td>An amber Combine elite marks a reachable junction with a square and cross. You have one second before it starts walking there. Take a different exit, interrupt it, or beat it to the junction. The marker deals no damage; its ordinary gunfire still warns before release.</td></tr></table><p>These specialists move through ordinary passages. Their plans are brief commitments to the position where they last saw a Hero, not knowledge of movement behind walls. Gates, obstructions, disabling conditions and interrupted plans can foil their maneuver.</p><p>Pincers appear beside Soldiers, Harriers beside Shamblers, and Waylayers beside Runners. Their route warnings remain visible with reduced effects. Generated class, health and elemental affinity still belong to the individual.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 50</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-reactions" data-title="Make their response your opening"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Make their response your opening</h2><table><tr><td>Pavise</td><td>The steel-blue Combine elite braces behind a shield-shaped tell. After a short warning, its guard stays aimed in one direction for 2.5 seconds. Frontal physical attacks face an extra Block chance, capped at 33%; it cannot walk or shoot while braced. Use Magic, circle to a flank, interrupt it, or wait for the guard to end. The ground arc shows the protected direction.</td></tr><tr><td>Repriser</td><td>The rose-colored Metrocop answers a direct damaging hit with a hooked-arrow warning and one straight projectile. Its one-second wind-up gives you time to sidestep, take cover or interrupt it with another damaging hit. It does not reflect your damage, and damage over time does not trigger the response.</td></tr><tr><td>Redliner</td><td>The rust-red Combine soldier normally fires from range. A direct hit that leaves it at 40% health or less can provoke one wounded rush: a jagged chevron announces the approach, followed by a one-second warning along its fixed lunge direction. Step aside or obstruct it. The lunge has three seconds of stationary recovery, shown by open bars; healing to 60% health rearms a later rush.</td></tr></table><p>Pavises appear with Runners, Reprisers with Soldiers, and Redliners with Shamblers. Watch both members of the pair: their different attack rhythms create different openings.</p><p>These responses keep ordinary class defenses, statuses and damage rules. Held stops the guard and lunge; Muted does not stop physical shots. Their warnings remain visible with reduced effects.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 51</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-patterns" data-title="Read the whole shot path"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Read the whole shot path</h2><table><tr><td>Caromer</td><td>The cyan Combine soldier draws a shot path with a diamond at its possible wall bank. After a 1.1-second warning, the physical projectile can ricochet once along the reflected line. Dodge the first lane and watch the second. New cover absorbs it; without a suitable wall it fires straight.</td></tr><tr><td>Reeler</td><td>The amber Metrocop warns along a fixed lane, then sends a shot beyond the position where it saw you. The shot pauses for three quarters of a second, marked by a hollow diamond, before returning to its original launch point. Sidestep, then stay clear until the return passes; cover stops either leg.</td></tr><tr><td>Forker</td><td>The mint-green Combine elite draws two parallel lanes for 1.2 seconds. Its paired shots leave the center open: hold that gap or move completely outside the lanes. There is no hidden middle shot. Cover and an interrupted wind-up can stop the volley.</td></tr></table><p>Caromers appear with Shamblers, Reelers with Runners, and Forkers with Soldiers. Companions can pressure the safe space, so read both attack rhythms. These are physical shots governed by ordinary class defenses and conditions.</p><p>Hit-stun can cancel the warning. A shot already in flight keeps its frozen path; it never follows a moving Hero. Each pattern can damage a given Hero at most once, including both Forker lanes. Reduced effects retain the path warnings.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 52</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-traps" data-title="Leave yourself an escape"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Leave yourself an escape</h2><table><tr><td>Wirewright</td><td>The cyan-and-orange Combine soldier marks a short tripwire across a lane. A hollow diamond and thin boundaries give 1.25 seconds of warning before it becomes live for five seconds. Endpoint posts and the upper rail show its jumpable height: jump above the wire, walk around either end, wait it out, or interrupt its maker. The narrow floor outline shows the full danger strip.</td></tr><tr><td>Snarer</td><td>The blue Vortigaunt arms a fixed circular snare after one second. It stays armed for at most six seconds. Entering with enough time left triggers one snap 1.25 seconds later, announced by inward teeth and a shrinking bar. Leave the circle to bait the snap safely; it cannot follow you or reset its timer. The Ice hit can leave you Held under the ordinary save rules. Mute, interrupt or defeat the Snarer to cancel it.</td></tr><tr><td>Cordon</td><td>The orange floor turret is a destructible pressure node. Two concentric rings warn for 1.5 seconds before one physical pulse, followed by a brief recovery. Enter the safe center, leave the outer ring, take cover, or interrupt or destroy the node to open the approach. The center stays safe; this is a single pulse, not lingering gas.</td></tr></table><p>Wirewrights appear with Runners, Snarers with Soldiers, and Cordons with Shamblers. Their companions pressure different escape routes; watch the whole encounter before committing.</p><p>These traps stay where they were marked. Thin boundaries and a hollow diamond mean arming; thicker boundaries mean live. Their finite warnings and escape routes remain visible with reduced effects.</p><p>A trap can hit a given Hero only once. Damage, defenses and conditions follow the ordinary combat rules. Interrupting or destroying the maker cancels its trap; removal never leaves a hidden hazard behind.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 53</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-melee-spacing" data-title="Respect the second swing"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Respect the second swing</h2><table><tr><td>Reaper</td><td>The green-tinted zombie marks a broad semicircle in front of itself for 1.1 seconds, then sweeps it once. Back beyond the arc, circle behind it, take cover or interrupt. Its facing stays fixed through the warning; exploit the 2.5-second stationary recovery.</td></tr><tr><td>Drubber</td><td>The ochre fast zombie marks two nested, narrow attack sectors. After one second it strikes the inner sector; 0.85 seconds later it strikes the longer outer sector along the same fixed direction. Sidestep and wait for both beats. Rushing straight back after the first swing can put you into the second. It then recovers for 2.8 seconds.</td></tr><tr><td>Fencer</td><td>The pale-violet Metrocop shows a dashed retreat path and steps backward, then warns for 0.9 seconds before a long, narrow thrust from its marked endpoint. Resist chasing straight after it; move sideways out of the rectangle. Obstructing the retreat or interrupting the warning stops the attack. It recovers for 2.5 seconds.</td></tr></table><p>Reapers appear with Soldiers, Drubbers with Runners, and Fencers with Shamblers. Their companions can contest the space you need to escape, so read the entire encounter.</p><p>All three make physical melee attacks under ordinary damage and defense rules. Interrupting them ends the commitment. Held stops a Fencer’s backstep but permits stationary strikes; Muted does not stop physical attacks. The fixed shapes and beat countdowns remain visible with reduced effects.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 54</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-remains" data-title="Watch what you leave behind"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Watch what you leave behind</h2><table><tr><td>Afterburst</td><td>The rust-orange zombie fights at close range. Defeating it leaves a fixed orange circle and shrinking bar around its corpse: after 0.8 seconds it bursts once. Back outside the circle or use cover before collecting its ordinary drop. Killing it stops its living attacks, but does not erase this warning.</td></tr><tr><td>Carrion</td><td>The green fast zombie can claim a nearby defeated ally when injured. A crossed-jaw mark, tether and shrinking bar announce 0.6 seconds of stationary feeding. Interrupt, kill or separate it, break the tether with cover, or defeat it before its Shambler escorts. A successful meal restores at most 30 HP, capped at maximum health. It gets one attempt per corpse and waits six seconds before another.</td></tr></table><p>An Afterburst appears with a Soldier; Carrion travels with Shamblers. Carrion can consume an Afterburst before it bursts, extinguishing the circle. Neither action creates another enemy or an extra reward; consumed remains still yield their ordinary drop on schedule.</p><p>Both enemies also warn a narrow, fixed melee sector. Sidestep or interrupt it. Held permits their stationary attacks and feeding; Muted does not stop these physical actions. Circles, tethers and countdowns remain readable with reduced effects.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 55</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-tether-cover" data-title="Break the line, cross the screen"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Break the line, cross the screen</h2><table><tr><td>Towline</td><td>The teal Metrocop marks a fixed narrow corridor and inward-pointing chevrons for 1.2 seconds. Sidestep, close inside 96 units, leave the lane or interrupt it. A successful physical hit can pull you a short distance toward it, subject to the ordinary push save. Blocked damage prevents the pull. Walls and missing floor support prevent movement. It then rests for three seconds.</td></tr><tr><td>Screenwright</td><td>The blue Combine engineer warns a slatted screen for one second, then holds it for three seconds. This screen is freely passable. It adds 25 percentage points to ordinary physical Block for up to three allies behind it when your attack crosses the screen; the combined chance still caps at 33%. Flank, walk through, use Magic, interrupt the engineer or wait. The engineer itself receives no protection from its screen. Without suitable allies it fires an ordinary physical shot.</td></tr></table><p>Towline appears with a Runner; Screenwright appears with a Soldier. Read the whole encounter: the companions can exploit a pull or shelter behind the projected screen. No extra bodies or rewards are created.</p><p>Both channels remain stationary and interruptible. Held and Muted do not prohibit these physical actions. Their frozen shapes and countdowns remain visible with reduced effects.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 56</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-mobile-hazards" data-title="Leave the route, mind the trail"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Leave the route, mind the trail</h2><table><tr><td>Censer</td><td>The amber Combine soldier outlines its entire short route for 1.2 seconds, then carries a dangerous 64-unit circle along it. Step sideways out of the outlined lane, take cover, interrupt it or defeat it. Once movement begins, the thick ring follows its actual position. The carrier stops at its destination or after 1.8 seconds; it cannot steer after you.</td></tr><tr><td>Trailmaker</td><td>The green Metrocop warns a short retreat route for 1.2 seconds. Dashed circles show where it may leave up to three 44-unit patches as it reaches those points. Each placed patch becomes a thin solid ring for another 0.8 seconds before turning thick and dangerous for 1.2 seconds. Leave the circles or wait; chasing directly along the trail is risky. Unreached points never become damaging patches.</td></tr></table><p>Each whole commitment can hit a given Hero at most once, for physical damage under ordinary defenses. These enemies choose routes with escape space; they cannot carry hazards through locked gates or unsupported floor. Their paths stay fixed and their hazards expire.</p><p>Interrupting, defeating or holding the source cancels the movement and every associated patch. Muted does not stop these physical actions. Cover blocks damage. Dashed plans, solid warnings, thick danger boundaries and shrinking countdowns remain visible with reduced effects.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 57</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-perception" data-title="Footsteps and sightlines"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Footsteps and sightlines</h2><table><tr><td>Listener</td><td>The amber Metrocop hears non-crouching footsteps in its own room. Its paired-ring marker and dashed route show a brief investigation toward that sound. Crouch, stop making footsteps, or change course after the cue. It does not track your hidden movement.</td></tr><tr><td>Shy</td><td>The violet fast zombie remembers a visible Hero, then advances toward that last-seen position only when no eligible Hero has line of sight to it. Its crossed-eye marker announces the fixed route. Keep a clear sightline, use a teammate to watch the lane, or relocate behind cover. Camera direction does not matter.</td></tr></table><p>Investigations warn for 0.8 seconds, travel at most 128 units, and end within another 1.4 seconds. Movement itself deals no damage. Listener needs fresh hearing and clear sight to strike; Shy can strike a visible Hero who comes close. Each melee attack has a separate 0.9-second, fixed narrow arc: leave it, use cover, or interrupt.</p><p>Their memory is brief and confined to a supported room. Invisibility prevents acquisition; they cannot follow you through walls, closed gates or missing floor. Interrupting or holding them cancels investigation; Muted does not silence physical footsteps or attacks. The dashed route, distinct destination glyph and countdown remain visible with reduced effects.</p><p>Listener is deliberately footstep-sensitive: gunfire and generic sound effects do not feed its hearing. A crouching Hero can still be threatened by its ordinary companions. Shy remains visible; its rule never forces you to aim or look in a particular direction.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 58</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-conditions" data-title="Cure the ailment, break the bargain"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Cure the ailment, break the bargain</h2><table><tr><td>Absolver</td><td>The pale-cyan slave Vortigaunt draws a crossed-diamond tether to an ailing ally and channels for 1.5 seconds. It removes one existing negative condition, without restoring HP. Interrupt or mute the Absolver, separate it from its ally, or break their sightline. A cure that has already happened cannot be taken back.</td></tr><tr><td>Exactor</td><td>The crimson Combine elite marks an ailing Hero’s position with a 64-unit circle and crossed diamond for 1.25 seconds. Leave the circle, cure the selected Bleeding, Immolated or Poisoned condition, break sight, or interrupt it. The mark never follows you. A successful physical hit consumes that ailment; ordinary Block and Dodge still apply. With no qualifying ailment, Exactor fires an ordinary warned shot.</td></tr></table><p>Absolver accompanies Shamblers; Exactor appears with a Flamer. Avoiding the Flamer’s Immolated attempt deprives Exactor of that opportunity. Exactor threatens only the Hero it marked, and each warning permits at most one hit.</p><p>Cleansing targets one condition in a fixed order, not every ailment. If that condition ends during either warning, the commitment fails; a new application needs a fresh warning. Neither enemy applies a new condition or creates extra bodies.</p><p>Held allows these stationary actions. Muted interrupts Absolver’s cleansing Magic but does not prevent either enemy’s physical shots. The tether, glyphs and shrinking countdowns remain visible with reduced effects.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 59</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-spacing" data-title="Together — or apart?"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Together — or apart?</h2><table><tr><td>Outrider</td><td>The sand-colored antlion singles out an isolated Hero. Its lone bracket glyph and narrow, frozen melee arc warn for 1.1 seconds. Bring an eligible teammate within 192 units on the same floor with clear sight to cancel the attack; alone, leave its arc, retreat or interrupt. The strike reaches 112 units and deals physical 1d6+2 before ordinary defenses.</td></tr><tr><td>Conductor</td><td>The pale-blue Vortigaunt marks exactly two nearby Heroes with linked 64-unit circles for 1.4 seconds. Separate beyond 160 units, leave either frozen circle, break its sight to either Hero or break the pair’s sightline to cancel the whole link. Each marked Hero risks one Raw Magic 1d6+2 hit. With no eligible pair, it fires an ordinary warned Raw projectile that a solo Hero can evade.</td></tr></table><p>Both attacks commit to the original Heroes and positions; replacement Heroes and later arrivals do not inherit a warning. No attack tracks hidden movement or adds a lasting field. Outrider recovers for 2.4 seconds after its attack.</p><p>Held permits these stationary attacks. Muted cancels Conductor’s Magic; Outrider’s physical strike remains available. Interrupting or defeating the source cancels its pending warning. The isolation glyph, arc, paired circles, linking line and countdowns remain visible with reduced effects.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 60</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-resources" data-title="Protect your Magic — interrupt theirs"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Protect your Magic — interrupt theirs</h2><table><tr><td>Siphoner</td><td>The violet Stalker marks a Hero’s position with a fixed 64-unit circle, downward funnel and 1.25-second countdown. Leave the circle, break its sight or interrupt or mute the caster. A hit deals Raw Magic 1d6+2 through ordinary defenses. Only a surviving Hero who actually loses HP also loses up to 12 current Magic; the Siphoner gains none. A fully absorbed, blocked or dodged hit has no additional drain. It recovers for 3.5 seconds.</td></tr><tr><td>Accumulator</td><td>The gold slave Vortigaunt marks a Hero with a fixed 64-unit circle and lightning glyph for 1.25 seconds. Its Raw Magic 1d6+2 attack spends 40 Magic before its ordinary cost modifiers. When it cannot afford that attack, a battery-and-plus glyph over its own position announces a two-second self-recharge instead. A completed recharge restores up to 45 Magic, capped at 100; interrupt or mute it, force it to move or break its sight to cancel the refill. It recovers for 2.5 seconds.</td></tr></table><p>Both resource attack marks threaten only the original Hero and never follow movement. Leave the circle or break sight before release; later arrivals and replacement lives do not inherit the mark. An Accumulator with an ordinary cast-triggered Aura Burst feat can also deal supplemental aura damage to other nearby Heroes, including those who escaped its circle. That separate feat never adds a Magic-drain rider. Arcane Shield and other defenses keep their ordinary behavior. These enemies do not steal loot, currency or permanent stats.</p><p>The Accumulator’s recharge creates no damaging circle and needs no ally: its battery glyph and countdown expose an opportunity to pressure it. Canceling a recharge still leaves its recovery period. Held permits stationary attacks and recharging; Muted cancels both enemies’ Magic. The drain, lightning and battery shapes and shrinking countdowns remain visible with reduced effects.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 61</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-crossfire" data-title="Turn their fire against them"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Turn their fire against them</h2><table><tr><td>Fusilier</td><td>The copper Combine soldier freezes a narrow firing lane for 1.25 seconds. Its arrow and body-bracket glyph warn where the single physical shot will go. Sidestep or place an ordinary enemy in the lane: the first body intercepts the shot, including one of its companions. Walls absorb it. The Fusilier then recovers for three seconds.</td></tr><tr><td>Bombardier</td><td>The ochre Combine elite marks a fixed 72-unit circle with a fragment glyph and 1.6-second countdown. Leave the circle and lure ordinary enemies into it: the physical blast can hit them too. A body cannot shield you from this area attack, but solid cover can. The circle stays where it was placed when you move away. The Bombardier then recovers for 3.5 seconds.</td></tr></table><p>Both attacks deal physical 1d6+2 before ordinary defenses and threaten only actors present when the warning began. Later arrivals and replacement lives do not inherit damage; an unmarked body can still absorb the Fusilier shot harmlessly. Neither enemy harms itself. Named bosses, their clones, event actors, friendly summons and human-controlled Soldiers are excluded from this friendly fire.</p><p>Interrupt, defeat or displace the attacker, or break its sight to cancel the warning. Held and Muted do not stop these stationary physical attacks; morale flight and hit-stun do. The firing lane, blast circle, distinct glyphs and countdowns remain visible with reduced effects. These two enemies do not make the rest of the prison fight among themselves.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 62</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-discipline" data-title="Stop — or keep moving"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Stop — or keep moving</h2><table><tr><td>Halter</td><td>The amber Combine elite tethers one Hero with an octagonal pause glyph and the literal command STOP. During the final 0.4 seconds, keep voluntary speed below one quarter of your legitimate walking speed.</td></tr><tr><td>Pacer</td><td>The cyan Metrocop tethers one Hero with double chevrons and KEEP MOVING. During the final 0.4 seconds, maintain voluntary speed at or above one quarter of your legitimate walking speed.</td></tr></table><p>Each demand warns for 1.6 seconds. PREPARE becomes JUDGMENT for the final 0.4 seconds; the countdown, thicker glyph and quarter-bar bracket identify that interval. A violating observation during judgment commits one physical 1d6+2 hit at release, subject to ordinary defenses. Comply throughout the interval to avoid it. Earlier movement does not decide the result.</p><p>The tether follows only the originally captured Hero life. Only one demand can hold a Hero at a time, including across both enemy types. Break sight with solid cover, leave the 360-unit range or current cell, or interrupt or defeat the source to cancel the demand. Later arrivals and replacement lives never inherit it.</p><p>Only actual voluntary motion counts: movement input against a wall is not enough for KEEP MOVING. Forced pushes, Held, nonwalking movement or stale motion evidence cancel the demand harmlessly, rather than making a forced failure count. An interval without enough reliable movement evidence also cancels.</p><p>Held and Muted on the attacker permit these stationary physical demands; morale flight and hit-stun cancel them. STOP and KEEP MOVING text, distinct glyphs, tether and countdown remain visible with reduced effects.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 63</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-companions" data-title="Break the bodyguard and the oath"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Break the bodyguard and the oath</h2><table><tr><td>Interposer</td><td>The blue Combine elite warns BODYGUARD for 0.8 seconds, then moves its own body along the frozen route toward an ally’s firing line. Its shield glyph stays on its actual body. Flank, change angle, use piercing or area attacks, interrupt, or defeat it. The route never follows your new position; its arrival hold lasts at most two seconds.</td></tr><tr><td>Mourner</td><td>The violet Metrocop warns OATH for 0.8 seconds, then binds one ordinary ally for three seconds. If that exact ally is legitimately defeated during the armed oath, the broken-oath glyph and RETALIATION announce a separate, full 1.2-second shot warning against the original Hero. Prioritize the Mourner, break the tether, wait out the oath, or defeat its ally and evade the warned shot.</td></tr></table><p>The Interposer grants no Block, immunity or damage diversion: ordinary body interception alone can protect the ward, so protection is never guaranteed. Held prevents its movement. The Mourner grants no resurrection or extra rewards; an old corpse cannot trigger its oath. The killer does not become a new retaliation target.</p><p>Without an eligible ally, either enemy warns SHOT for 1.2 seconds. Every fallback or retaliation shot freezes the original Hero’s visible position when that shot warning begins, then releases one physical 1d6+2 packet before ordinary defenses. Leave the fixed lane, use cover or another body, or interrupt the attacker. The first actual body or wall blocks the shot; an intervening enemy is not damaged by it. Each attack ends in three seconds of recovery.</p><p>Only one bodyguard or oath can reserve an ordinary ally at a time. Breaking sight, leaving range, displacing the stationary source or ward, or defeating the source cancels the commitment. Held permits stationary oaths and shots; Muted permits these physical actions. Morale flight and hit-stun cancel them. BODYGUARD, OATH, RETALIATION and SHOT labels, route or lane, distinct shield or oath glyphs and countdowns remain visible with reduced effects.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 64</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-edicts" data-title="Cease fire or find refuge"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Cease fire or find refuge</h2><table><tr><td>Censor</td><td>The ochre Combine soldier marks one Hero with a crossed-barrel glyph and CEASE FIRE. PREPARE lasts 0.8 seconds; WATCH lasts 2.4 seconds. Starting a firearm, crowbar, Magic or Wand attack during WATCH triggers a separate, full 1.2-second RETALIATION warning. Stop attacking through WATCH, interrupt or defeat the Censor, break sight with solid cover, or leave range.</td></tr><tr><td>Surveyor</td><td>The mint Vortigaunt freezes a 144-unit threat ring around your initial position for 1.6 seconds. REFUGE marks a safe pocket of radius 48, displaced 96 units from the ring center. Enter that pocket or LEAVE RING before judgment. Both routes are checked for your actual body when the warning starts. The rings and arrows stay fixed when you move.</td></tr></table><p>The Censor observes new committed attacks, not damage: passive effects, ailments, earlier projectiles and equipment actions that do not commit an attack cannot trigger it. Retaliation freezes your visible position when its new warning begins. Leave the fixed lane, use a wall or intervening body, or interrupt the source. The first body or solid cover absorbs the shot; an intervening enemy is not damaged. The single physical hit is 1d6+2 before ordinary defenses.</p><p>The Surveyor releases one physical 1d6+2 hit only if the originally marked Hero remains inside the outer ring and outside the refuge at judgment. Solid cover, leaving range, interrupting or defeating the Surveyor also cancels it. Muted cancels Surveyor judgment; Held does not. Other enemies can still threaten either escape route: refuge protects only against this judgment.</p><p>Each commitment belongs to the original Hero life. A death, replacement life, reset or invalid geometry cancels it; nobody else inherits the warning. Held and Muted permit the Censor’s stationary physical actions. Morale flight and hit-stun interrupt both enemies. Three seconds of recovery follow each commitment. Labels, distinct glyphs, fixed geometry and countdowns remain visible with reduced effects.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 65</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-links" data-title="Break the relay and the living ribbon"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Break the relay and the living ribbon</h2><table><tr><td>Relay</td><td>The ice-blue Combine soldier draws a linked-diamond channel to one ordinary ally and a separate arrow from that ally toward your frozen position. RELAY SHOT warns for 1.4 seconds. The shot comes from the ally’s captured position, so watch the far end of the channel. Sidestep the fixed shot, interpose a body or solid cover, or defeat either endpoint.</td></tr><tr><td>Lacemaker</td><td>The rose Metrocop draws a ground ribbon to one ordinary ally. LEAVE RIBBON warns for 1.2 seconds before the ribbon becomes ACTIVE for two seconds. Its double edge encloses an 18-unit radius. The source stays still while the ally continues its own ordinary movement: bait the ally to rotate the line away, leave the ribbon, break the tether with cover, or defeat either endpoint.</td></tr></table><p>The Relay’s arrow never tracks your new position or the ally’s movement. The first body or solid wall absorbs its single physical 1d6+2 shot before ordinary defenses; intervening enemies receive no damage from it. Moving the ally more than 32 units from its captured position cancels the channel.</p><p>The Lacemaker’s live ribbon can deliver at most one physical 1d6+2 hit to the originally captured Hero, subject to ordinary ]====]
